invariant: INV-26
file: ghash_hw.c
catches: ghash_equiv_test
reason: the AES=hw reduction adds the upper half shifted right by 8 where
  the x^7 term of x^128 + x^7 + x^2 + x + 1 needs a shift by 7, so every
  product with a nonzero upper half is reduced modulo the wrong
  polynomial and GHASH answers wrong under AES=hw alone. CBMC cannot read
  an intrinsic, so no proof covers this file, and bin/ghash_equiv_test
  is what holds it to gcm.c's portable multiply: it compares the
  multiply, the loop over data and the whole AEAD byte for byte. The
  edit lands in reduce, which is the same C after either instruction, so
  it compiles on an ARMv8 host and an x86-64 one alike and the catch does
  not depend on which instruction set the runner has.

--- old
        s->product[3] ^ upper_high ^ (upper_high >> 1) ^ (upper_high >> 2) ^ (upper_high >> 7);
--- new
        s->product[3] ^ upper_high ^ (upper_high >> 1) ^ (upper_high >> 2) ^ (upper_high >> 8);
